%[text] # Ansys Automation
%[text] **J.G. Vermond, JVsound**
%[text] How to create, from MATLAB, the text files with the FEA pressures that `comp.FeaEnclosure` reads, from a Workbench project in Ansys. The results serve as reference data for the tests. Status of 2026-09-29.
%%
%[text] ## Overview
%[text] A live script of the test, such as `createFeaResults`, describes the job and calls `exportFeaPressureResults`. That function always does its work in a second MATLAB in the background, so MATLAB stays free: it starts the second MATLAB, which calls the same function again and then calls `readAnsysFea` for each system of the Workbench project. `readAnsysFea` runs the Python script `solveFea.py`, which solves the system with PyWorkbench when it has no result file yet, and reads the pressures with PyDPF. All results stay in the Workbench project. The three files, and the source of the flowchart (`flowchart.svg`), are in `helperfiles`, next to this page.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:3c7e91a2)
%%
%[text] ## Computer setup
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[160,-1]}
%[text] | Item | Value |
%[text] | --- | --- |
%[text] | Ansys | Ansys Student 2026 R1 (v261); `AWP_ROOT261` points to its folder; 128k nodes, 4 cores |
%[text] | Python | 3.13 from python.org, virtual environment `C:\Users\Jeroen Vermond\venvs\pyansys` |
%[text] | PyAnsys | `ansys-workbench-core` and `ansys-dpf-core` in that environment |
%[text] | MATLAB | R2026a, with `pyenv` set once to the Python of the environment |
%[text:table]
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] pyenv(Version="C:\Users\Jeroen Vermond\venvs\pyansys\Scripts\python.exe",ExecutionMode="OutOfProcess");
%[text] ```
%[text] With a new Ansys release, update the packages (`pip install --upgrade ...`) and `ANSYS_VERSION` in `solveFea.py`.
%%
%[text] ## Working method
%[text] 1. Prepare each model in Workbench and Mechanical: geometry, mesh, boundary conditions and named selections. Save the project and close Workbench.
%[text] 2. Describe the job in the live script of the test, as a structure with the fields `WorkbenchFile` (the `.wbpj`), `Models` (subfolder and system folder, for example `"full200l","SYS"`), `Selections` (named selection and file name) and `OutFolder`.
%[text] 3. Run the live script. `exportFeaPressureResults(job)` saves the job to `scratch\exportFeaPressureResults.mat`, starts the second MATLAB and returns at once. The second MATLAB writes its output to `scratch\exportFeaPressureResults.log`, with a line per finished model. \
%[text] Mind:
%[text] - A system is solved only when it has no result file `<project>_files\dp0\SYS-<n>\MECH\file.rst`. To solve a system again, clear its generated data in Workbench and save the project.
%[text] - Workbench must be closed while a system is solved: the project is locked while it is open.
%[text] - The named selections are found by name; capitals do not matter. A missing one stops the run with an error.
%[text] - With 250 frequencies a full model of the closed box takes about 20 minutes and a quarter-symmetry model about 3 minutes; reading takes a few seconds. \
%%
%[text] ## What was found
%[text] - The frequency responses of Mechanical (`AcousticPressureFrequencyResponse`) cannot be exported by a script in 2026 R1, so the pressures are read from the result file with PyDPF: full precision, and the unit comes with the data.
%[text] - The plain mean over the nodes of a named selection equals the frequency response of Mechanical with Spatial Resolution Use Average.
%[text] - PyWorkbench runs Workbench script code with `run_script_string`. Its `args` option fails on the backslashes of a Windows path, so `solveFea.py` fills in the path itself, with forward slashes. The system with folder `SYS-1` is called `SYS 1` in that code.
%[text] - Solving a copy of the model with PyMechanical also works, but then the results are not in the Workbench project; that route was dropped on 2026-09-29. \

